    "q-71091": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71091",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the intervals where the function $F(t)=f(t)\\cdot g(t)$ is differentiable, given that\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{t}^{3},k \\\\ g(t) & =({t}^{2}-4)\\,i+\\sqrt{t+5}\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\cdot g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A vector function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-t)$ is differentiable on $(-\\infty ,2).$\n\n- The second and third components, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and ${t}^{3},$ are differentiable for all values of $t.$\n\nSo,  the intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,2).$  \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first and third components, ${t}^{2}-4$  and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ are differentiable for all values of $t.$\n\n- The second component $\\sqrt{t+5}$ is differentiable on $(-5,\\infty )$, as its derivative is not defined at $t=-5$ and the function is not defined for $t<-5.$\n\nHence,  the intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is  $(-5,\\infty ).$    \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ is $(-\\infty ,2)\\cap (-5,\\infty )=(-5,2).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
