    "q-127853": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127853",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2),\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt{3-t}⟩$ is differentiable.",
      "worked_solution": "The vector-valued function $f(t)$ is defined on the interval $(-2,3].$ Let's study the differentiability of its components on this interval one-by-one.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2)$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2))}^{′}=\\frac{1}{t+2}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $(-2,3],$ we conclude that  $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- The derivative of  ${t}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{({t}^{2})}^{′}=2t.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is defined for all the points on the interval $(-2,3],$ we conclude that  ${t}^{2}$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- The derivative of  $\\sqrt{3-t}$ is\n\n$$\n{(\\sqrt{3-t})}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3-t}}.\n$$\n\n \nSince the derivative is not defined at $t=3,$ we conclude that  $\\sqrt{3-t}$ is not differentiable at this point.\n\nTaking into account that all the components of $f(t)$ are differentiable for all the points on the interval $(-2,3]$ except the point $t=3,$ we conclude that $f(t)$  is differentiable on $(-2,3).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
